from pathlib import Path
import sys,math
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('26');add=A.add
add('5.7.2.1-01','A changing magnetic linkage can induce an e.m.f. in a coil.','Current follows only when a suitable conducting path is complete.','Connect a coil to a sensitive centre-zero meter and move a magnet into it. The meter deflects during motion; holding the magnet stationary gives no sustained induction from that unchanged arrangement. Withdraw it and the deflection reverses. Moving the coil instead can give a similar relative-change effect. Use a low-energy classroom apparatus with no external supply connected to the sensing coil.','Describe an experiment demonstrating induced e.m.f. with a magnet and coil.','Move a magnet into and out of a meter-connected coil and compare the opposite transient deflections with the stationary case.')
add('5.7.2.1-02','A greater rate of change of magnetic linkage gives a greater induced e.m.f. in the qualitative model.','Faster motion, stronger magnetic field or more linked coil turns can increase the effect in a suitable comparison.','Hold the other factors fixed when testing one. Moving a magnet faster can increase the meter pulse, but a continuously stationary strong magnet does not provide a sustained induced output by strength alone. Coil orientation and the part of the field crossed also matter.','State one change that can increase a magnet-coil induced e.m.f. with other conditions fixed.','Move the magnet faster relative to the coil.')
add('5.7.2.1-03','Lenz’s law gives an induced current direction that opposes the change producing it.','The opposing effect is consistent with the need for energy input during generation.','If a north pole approaches a coil, the induced current can make the near face north and oppose approach. When the pole withdraws, the induced face changes so it opposes withdrawal. The current does not oppose an unchanged field by automatically cancelling all of it; it opposes the change.','Explain the induced magnetic effect when a north pole approaches a closed coil.','The induced current makes the near coil face act as north.~Its repulsion opposes the approaching change, as required by Lenz’s law.')
add('5.7.2.2-01','An alternating generator uses changing magnetic linkage caused by mechanical rotation.','Either a coil rotates in a field or a magnet rotates relative to stationary coils.','The output polarity changes as the relative orientation changes. A rotating-coil arrangement can use continuous slip rings to connect each coil end to stationary brushes. A rotating-magnet arrangement can leave output coils stationary and avoid that particular output contact requirement. Mechanical input is the energy source for the electrical transfer.','Explain why rotating-coil and rotating-magnet arrangements can both generate alternating output.','Link both to periodic change in magnetic linkage through a coil.~The changing orientation reverses the induced polarity periodically.')
add('5.7.2.2-01-M01','A rotating-coil AC generator has a coil driven mechanically in a magnetic field.','Its two ends connect to separate continuous slip rings and brushes in the simple model.','As the coil turns, its linked magnetic field changes and an e.m.f. is induced. The direction reverses each half-turn in the basic two-pole design. A complete external circuit allows current; an open circuit can have voltage without appreciable load current.','Describe a simple rotating-coil AC generator.','Rotate a coil in a magnetic field and collect its alternating output through separate slip rings and stationary brushes.')
add('5.7.2.2-01-M02','A rotating-magnet generator changes the field through stationary coils.','The output can be taken directly from the stationary winding connections.','A north and south pole moving past a coil change the direction and size of its magnetic linkage. Continued rotation gives periodic output. The number of poles affects cycles per mechanical turn, so the one-cycle-per-turn graph here belongs to the simple two-pole model.','Describe how a rotating magnet generates alternating output in a stationary coil.','Drive the magnet past the coil repeatedly so its changing field induces a periodically reversing e.m.f.')
add('5.7.2.2-01-M03','Each continuous slip ring maintains connection to the same rotating coil end.','Unlike a split-ring commutator, this arrangement does not interchange the coil connections every half-turn.','Stationary brushes rub on the rings and pass the coil’s alternating polarity to the outside circuit. The rings are separate conductors attached to the two ends, not one shared shorted contact. Compare their connection role with the DC motor’s split-ring commutation rather than memorising only the names.','Describe the role of slip rings in a rotating-coil AC generator.','Separate continuous rings connect each coil end to a stationary brush without swapping the ends, preserving the alternating output.')
add('5.7.2.2-02','A simple uniformly rotating two-pole generator can produce an approximately sinusoidal voltage-time output.','Faster rotation at unchanged field and coil raises peak voltage and reduces period in the ideal comparison.','A positive half-cycle is followed by a negative one. Doubling rotation frequency doubles electrical frequency in the simple two-pole model; the period halves. The faster magnetic-linkage change also raises e.m.f. amplitude. This is a voltage graph: its vertical values are not the angular speed of the coil.','Explain using a voltage-time graph the effect of faster rotation in a simple generator.','Link faster rotation to a shorter output period and higher frequency.~The more rapid linkage change also gives a larger voltage amplitude at unchanged coil and field.')
add('5.7.2.2-02-M01','An AC generator’s voltage alternates positive and negative relative to a chosen terminal convention.','A full cycle includes both signs and returns to the same phase.','Use an invented model with peak 2 V and period 1 s over 0–2 s. Draw two complete smooth cycles about a zero-voltage axis. A plot staying positive is not the same alternating waveform, even if its size varies. Mark both axes and the negative part of the scale.','Draw an idealised generator output with peak 2 V and period 1 s over 0–2 s.','Put time / s horizontally and voltage / V vertically.~Draw two sinusoidal cycles spanning +2 V to −2 V about zero.')
add('5.7.2.2-02-M02','In the ideal fixed-field two-pole model, doubling rotation rate halves period and increases output amplitude.','The comparison must keep coil geometry, turns and field unchanged.','Compare the 2 V, 1 s waveform with an illustrative 4 V, 0.5 s waveform. In the same time interval the second has twice as many complete cycles. Load and resistance can alter real terminal behaviour, so these are unloaded idealised traces for the stated comparison.','Draw the comparison output when the ideal 2 V peak, 1 s period generator doubles rotation rate with other conditions fixed.','Use the same axes and draw a 4 V peak waveform with 0.5 s period.~It has twice as many cycles in the same time interval.')
x=[i/100 for i in range(201)]
for key in ['5.7.2.2-02','5.7.2.2-02-M01','5.7.2.2-02-M02']:A.plot(key,x,{'Original rate':[2*math.sin(2*math.pi*t) for t in x],'Double rate':[4*math.sin(4*math.pi*t) for t in x]},'Time / s','Voltage / V','Idealised fixed-field two-pole generator')
add('5.7.2.2-03','A generator supplies current when its induced e.m.f. acts through a complete external circuit.','Mechanical work supplies the energy transferred electrically.','Turning a loaded generator commonly requires greater input effort because the induced current’s magnetic effect opposes the producing change. The output does not come from consuming the permanent magnet’s material. Disconnecting a load can reduce current while the rotating generator still has an open-circuit e.m.f.','Describe how a simple generator produces current in a load.','Drive the coil or magnet mechanically and connect a complete external load path so the induced e.m.f. drives current.')
add('5.7.2.3-01','A transformer has primary and secondary coils linked by a magnetic core.','Changing primary current produces a changing core field that induces secondary e.m.f.','An iron core provides effective linkage, and laminations reduce unwanted circulating-current heating in a real design. A steady DC input gives no sustained secondary output after the switching transient in this simple transformer. The two windings need not be electrically joined for energy transfer, because their link is the changing field.','Describe how an iron-cored transformer transfers energy between coils.','Alternating primary current changes the core field, which links the secondary and induces an alternating e.m.f. there.')
add('5.7.2.3-02','For an ideal transformer, Vs/Vp = Ns/Np.','A secondary with more turns steps voltage up; fewer turns steps it down.','For primary 100 turns at 6 V and secondary 50 turns, the ideal output is 3 V. The ratios use corresponding primary and secondary positions. The practical graph of secondary voltage against secondary turns has slope Vp/Np when primary conditions are fixed.','Calculate Vs for Vp = 12 V, Np = 240 and Ns = 60 in an ideal transformer.','Vs = VpNs/Np = 12 × 60/240.~The secondary voltage is 3 V.')
add('5.7.2.3-03','Higher transmission voltage permits lower current for the same transferred power in the simple comparison.','Lower current reduces resistive line heating for the same line resistance.','The familiar line-loss relation varies with the square of current, so reducing current can substantially reduce unwanted transfer to the wires. A step-up transformer is used near an AC generation source and step-down stages near consumers. High voltage also requires appropriate insulation and clearances; a lower loss does not make it safe for direct use.','State the main energy advantage of high-voltage transmission at fixed transmitted power.','It lowers current and therefore reduces resistive heating losses in the lines.')
add('5.7.2.3-04','Underground cables reduce visual impact and some exposure to weather compared with overhead lines.','They commonly cost more to install and can make fault location, repair and heat removal more difficult.','Overhead routes need towers and clearances and affect landscape and land use. Underground routes require excavation and can disturb soil and habitats during construction. Compare the named route, terrain and maintenance requirements rather than making a universal cost statement for every project.','Explain one benefit and one drawback of underground rather than overhead transmission.','Underground cables reduce visible structures and some weather exposure.~They commonly require more costly installation and more difficult access for repairs.')
add('5.7.2.3-05','An ideal 100%-efficient transformer has equal input and output power.','For the stated model VpIp = VsIs, so voltage increase is accompanied by current decrease for the same power.','A 12 V, 0.50 A primary input is 6 W. At an ideal 3 V secondary output, current is 2 A. Real transformers have losses, so output power is smaller and the ideal equality must not be applied as an exact measured fact. Transformer voltage multiplication is not power creation.','Calculate Is for an ideal transformer with Vp = 12 V, Ip = 0.50 A and Vs = 3 V.','Is = VpIp/Vs = 12 × 0.50/3.~The secondary current is 2 A.')
add('5.7.2.3-06','An AC generation-to-consumer pathway uses transformers at transmission and distribution stages.','The transformer role is to provide high-voltage transmission and lower-voltage distribution.','An AC generator feeds a step-up transformer before transmission lines; step-down transformers later supply lower voltages to consumers. No named plant or current electricity-system status is asserted.','Describe the roles of transformers between an AC generator and consumers.','A step-up stage raises voltage for transmission, while step-down stages lower voltage for distribution and consumers.',heading='Generation, transmission and distribution stages')
C=A.choice
C('5.7.2.1-01','A magnet is held stationary inside a stationary coil. What is expected after transients?',('No sustained induced e.m.f. from the unchanged field','Induction requires changing linkage.'),('A sustained output solely because field exists','Field presence alone is insufficient.'),('An increasing voltage without input','No changing linkage is specified.'),('The coil becomes a cell','Induction is not chemical-cell action.'))
C('5.7.2.1-03','What does Lenz’s law oppose?',('The change causing induction','This is its directional rule.'),('Every existing field by complete cancellation','The rule concerns change.'),('Only the coil’s weight','That is not the magnetic change.'),('Energy conservation','It is consistent with conservation.'))
C('5.7.2.2-01-M03','What preserves connection to each rotating AC coil end?',('Separate continuous slip rings','They do not interchange coil ends.'),('One short circuit joining both ends','That would short the output.'),('A split ring used for DC commutation','It changes connections each half-turn.'),('Removing all brushes without another contact method','The output path would be absent.'))
C('5.7.2.3-01','What supplies the changing core field in a simple transformer?',('Alternating primary current','It creates changing linkage.'),('Steady DC indefinitely','After the transient it gives no sustained change.'),('No input of any kind','Energy transfer requires input.'),('A stationary unchanging field alone','It does not induce steady secondary output.'))
C('5.7.2.3-03','At fixed power, why raise transmission voltage?',('To lower current and line heating','The reduced current reduces resistive loss.'),('To increase current by definition','P = VI gives the opposite at fixed power.'),('To create power','Transformers do not create energy.'),('To remove insulation needs','Higher voltage requires suitable insulation.'))
C('5.7.2.3-04','Which is a common underground-cable advantage?',('Less visual impact','Large overhead structures are avoided along the cable route.'),('No excavation needed','Underground installation normally needs ground work.'),('Guaranteed zero repair cost','Fault access can be difficult.'),('No need for insulation','Cables require insulation.'))
C('5.7.2.2-03','What energy input drives a generator?',('Mechanical input','Rotation requires work.'),('Charge destruction','Charge is conserved.'),('Permanent magnet consumption as fuel','That is not the intended mechanism.'),('No input after connecting a load','A load does not supply free energy.'))
C('5.7.2.3-02','Np = 200, Ns = 100 and Vp = 8 V. What is ideal Vs?',('4 V','The secondary has half the turns.'),('16 V','This reverses the ratio.'),('8 V','Turns differ.'),('0.5 V','That is the turns fraction without applying voltage.'))
C('5.7.2.3-05','An ideal transformer supplies 12 W at 3 V. What output current flows?',('4 A','Use I = P/V.'),('36 A','This multiplies power and voltage.'),('0.25 A','This reverses the ratio.'),('12 A','This ignores voltage.'))
C('5.7.2.2-02-M02','Rotation rate doubles in the fixed-field two-pole model. What happens to period?',('It halves','Frequency doubles.'),('It doubles','This reverses the frequency relation.'),('It stays fixed','The repetition rate changed.'),('It becomes amplitude','These are different quantities.'))
C('5.7.2.1-01','The magnet’s motion through a closed coil is reversed. What changes?',('Induced polarity reverses','The linkage change has the opposite sense.'),('Polarity must stay fixed','The change direction matters.'),('Charge is created','Charge flows rather than being created.'),('The coil loses its turns','The geometry can stay unchanged.'))
C('5.7.2.3-02','At fixed primary conditions, secondary turns double. What is ideal output voltage?',('Twice as large','Vs scales directly with Ns under the fixed primary conditions.'),('Half as large','That reverses the turns effect.'),('Four times as large','The ideal relation is linear.'),('Unchanged','The turns ratio changed.'))
P=A.part
P('5.7.2.2-01-M03','Name the rotating contact arrangement in the simple AC coil generator.','Slip rings.',1,'One mark for continuous slip rings.')
P('5.7.2.3-01','State why steady DC is unsuitable for sustained output from the simple transformer.','It gives no continuing change of core field after the transient.',1,'One mark for absence of changing linkage.')
P('5.7.2.3-03','Explain the loss advantage of higher transmission voltage at fixed power.','Current is lower. Resistive heating in the same line is reduced.',2,'One mark for lower current; one for lower heating.')
P('5.7.2.3-02','Calculate Vs for Vp = 10 V, Np = 200 and Ns = 80.','Vs = 10 × 80/200 = 4 V.',2,'One mark for turns ratio; one for 4 V.')
P('5.7.2.3-05','Calculate secondary current when ideal input power is 8 W and Vs = 4 V.','Is = P/Vs = 2 A.',2,'One mark for power divided by voltage; one for 2 A.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot secondary voltage vertically against secondary turn count horizontally at fixed primary conditions.',unit='V per turn',meaning='The slope is Vp/Np in the ideal model: voltage per secondary turn. It is not transformer efficiency, which needs input and output power.',method=['Use a purpose-built low-voltage AC transformer kit with a fixed primary winding.','Set and measure a safe fixed primary voltage without using exposed mains connections.','Fit or select a known secondary turn count and measure its open-circuit AC voltage.','Repeat for several secondary turn counts with the same assembled core and primary conditions.','Repeat readings and check primary voltage remains stable.'],control='Use rated insulated classroom equipment, keep the core assembly consistent, avoid shorted turns and switch off before changing windings.',table='Record secondary turns, primary voltage / V and secondary voltage / V, including repeats.',conclusion='A straight near-origin secondary-voltage versus turns line supports the ideal turns relation. Its slope gives voltage per turn; efficiency cannot be inferred from these open-circuit voltages alone.')
